slug: six-minute-walk-test-breathlessness
title: The Six-Minute Walk Test — Measuring Breathlessness Safely
area: Skills · Vitals & Monitoring
level: Clinical
best: 2nd year onwards
minutes: 12
hook: Walking for six minutes tells you more about a patient's daily life than many lab tests. Learn to run the test exactly the same way every time, watch the oxygen level and breathlessness, and know when to stop. || Chhe minute chalna rozmarra zindagi ke baare mein bohat si lab tests se zyada batata hai. Test ko har baar bilkul ek hi tareeqe se karna, oxygen aur saans ki tangi dekhna aur rukne ka waqt jaanna seekhein.
goal: By the end, you'll prepare and run a standard six-minute walk test, record distance, oxygen saturation, heart rate and the Borg breathlessness score, and decide whether a change is real. || End tak aap standard chhe minute walk test taiyar karenge aur chalayenge, distance, oxygen saturation, heart rate aur Borg saans ki tangi ka score likhenge, aur faisla karenge ke tabdeeli asli hai ya nahi.

say: The test asks one simple question: **how far can the patient walk in six minutes at their own pace?** It is a **submaximal** test, so the patient may slow down or rest, and the clock keeps running. Because the result depends on how you run it, the setup must be the same each time: a **straight, flat corridor of 30 metres**, the same instructions and the same **standard encouragement**. || Test ek saada sawal poochta hai: **patient apni raftar se chhe minute mein kitna chal sakta hai?** Yeh **submaximal** test hai, is liye patient ruk sakta hai ya aahista ho sakta hai aur ghari chalti rehti hai. Nateeja is par depend karta hai ke aap kaise chalate hain, is liye setup har baar ek jaisa ho: **30 meter ka seedha, hamwar corridor**, wohi hidayat aur wohi **standard hausla afzai**.
analogy: Think of the test as a car road test on the same road each time. If you change the road, the weather or the instructions, you cannot say whether the car got better. In the same way, a change in distance only means something if the corridor, the encouragement, the walking aid and the time of day stay the same. || Test ko ek hi sadak par gaari ka road test samjhein. Agar aap sadak, mausam ya hidayat badal dein to nahi keh sakte ke gaari behtar hui. Isi tarah distance mein tabdeeli tabhi maani rakhti hai jab corridor, hausla afzai, chalne ka sahara aur waqt ek jaise rahein.
steps: Running the test || Test chalana
step: Check before you start :: Ask about chest pain, recent heart attack, severe breathlessness at rest, and check resting pulse, blood pressure and oxygen. Do not start if the unit's safety limits are crossed. || Shuru karne se pehle check karein :: Seene ka dard, haal ki dil ki bimari, aaram mein shadeed saans ki tangi poochhein aur aaram ka pulse, blood pressure aur oxygen check karein. Unit ki hifazati hadood paar hon to shuru na karein.
step: Prepare the patient :: Comfortable clothes and shoes, usual walking aid, light meal only, rest sitting for ten minutes. Record the Borg breathlessness score and heart rate at rest. || Patient ko taiyar karein :: Aaram deh kapray aur joote, aam chalne ka sahara, halka khana, das minute baith kar aaram. Aaram mein Borg saans ki tangi ka score aur heart rate likhein.
step: Give the instructions :: Say the standard words: walk as far as you can in six minutes; you may slow down, stop and rest, and then start again when able. Use only the standard encouragement each minute. || Hidayat dein :: Standard alfaz kahein: chhe minute mein jitna door chal sakein chalein; aap aahista ho sakte hain, ruk kar aaram kar sakte hain aur phir dobara shuru kar sakte hain. Har minute sirf standard hausla afzai dein.
step: Record and compare :: Note the total distance, the lowest oxygen, the end Borg score and heart rate, and any stops. Compare with last time, the same conditions, and with the expected value. || Likhein aur muqabla karein :: Kul distance, sab se kam oxygen, aakhir ka Borg score aur heart rate aur ruknay ki tafseel likhein. Pichli dafa se (ek jaisi sharait mein) aur mutawaqqa qeemat se muqabla karein.

check: Which of these should be the same every time the test is repeated? || Test dohrate waqt inme se kya har baar ek jaisa hona chahiye?
ok: Corridor length, instructions and encouragement || Corridor ki lambai, hidayat aur hausla afzai || Yes — standardisation is what makes a change in distance meaningful. || Haan — standardisation hi distance ki tabdeeli ko maani deti hai.
no: The patient's mood || Patient ka mood || That cannot be controlled. || Yeh qaboo nahi kiya ja sakta.
no: The encouragement, chosen freely each time || Hausla afzai jo har baar apni marzi se ho || Free encouragement changes the result. || Azaad hausla afzai nateeja badal deti hai.
compare: Borg scale or oxygen saturation? || Borg scale ya oxygen saturation?
head: Feature || Khasiyat ;; Borg breathlessness score || Borg saans ki tangi ka score ;; Pulse oximeter (oxygen) || Pulse oximeter (oxygen)
row: What it measures || Kya naapta hai ;; How hard breathing feels to the patient || Patient ko saans lena kitna mushkil lagta hai ;; The oxygen carried in the blood || Khoon mein oxygen ki miqdar
row: When you read it || Kab dekhte hain ;; Before, at the end and when asked || Pehle, aakhir mein aur poochne par ;; Before, during and at the end || Pehle, dauran aur aakhir mein
row: A limit of the measure || Is ki hadd ;; It is the patient's own feeling || Yeh patient ka apna ehsaas hai ;; Cold hands or a poor signal give false low readings || Thande hath ya kamzor signal ghalat kam reading dete hain
row: Use in the decision || Faisle mein istemal ;; Shows how hard the walk was || Batata hai ke chalna kitna mushkil tha ;; Shows whether to stop or refer || Batata hai ke rukna ya refer karna hai ya nahi
memory: **Same road, same words.** A six-minute walk result is only as good as how closely you repeated the conditions. Write down the corridor, the aid, the oxygen use and the time of day beside the number. || **Wohi sadak, wohi alfaz.** Chhe minute walk ka nateeja utna hi achha hai jitni ek jaisi sharait aap ne dohrayi. Number ke saath corridor, sahara, oxygen ka istemal aur waqt likhein.
mistake: **Walking with the patient and setting the pace.** If you walk beside them, you change their speed. Stand at the end of the corridor, say the standard words and let the patient choose the pace. || **Patient ke saath chal kar raftar tay karna.** Agar aap saath chalein to unki raftar badal jati hai. Corridor ke ek sire par khare hon, standard alfaz kahein aur patient ko apni raftar chunne dein.
check: A patient's distance rises from 280 to 295 metres after rehabilitation. What is the best conclusion? || Rehabilitation ke baad patient ka distance 280 se 295 meter hua. Behtareen nateeja kya hai?
ok: The change is small and may be within the test's normal variation; look at symptoms too || Tabdeeli chhoti hai aur test ki aam tabdeeli ke andar ho sakti hai; alamaat bhi dekhein || Yes — in chronic lung disease a change of about 30 metres is usually needed to be sure it is real. || Haan — phephron ki purani bimari mein tabdeeli ko pakka karne ke liye aam taur par takreeban 30 meter chahiye.
no: The treatment has clearly worked || Ilaj saaf taur par kamyab hua || The change is too small to be sure. || Tabdeeli itni chhoti hai ke yaqeen na ho.
no: The test must be wrong || Test ghalat hona chahiye || Not necessarily; the change is just small. || Zaroori nahi; tabdeeli bas chhoti hai.
check: Which finding means you should stop the test? || Kaun si finding batati hai ke test rok dein?
ok: Chest pain, severe breathlessness, dizziness or a pale, sweaty patient || Seene ka dard, shadeed saans ki tangi, chakkar ya zard paseene mein dooba patient || Yes — these are the standard stopping criteria; sit the patient down and monitor. || Haan — yeh rukne ke standard mayaar hain; patient ko bithayein aur nigrani karein.
no: A mild rise in heart rate || Heart rate ka halka izafa || That is expected during walking. || Chalne ke dauran yeh mutawaqqa hai.
no: The patient asking about the time || Patient ka waqt poochna || That is not a reason to stop. || Yeh rukne ki wajah nahi.
clinic: **Plan with the number.** Use the distance and Borg score to set exercise intensity: many programmes ask patients to train at a breathlessness of three to four on the Borg scale. Record the same test at the start, mid-point and end so progress is clear to the patient. || **Number ke saath plan banayein.** Distance aur Borg score se exercise ki shiddat tay karein: bohat se programmes patient se Borg par teen se chaar ki saans ki tangi par training karwate hain. Shuru, beech aur end mein wohi test likhein taake patient ko progress saaf nazar aaye.
reveal: Why is the six-minute walk test called submaximal? || Chhe minute walk test ko submaximal kyun kehte hain? || Because the patient chooses their own pace and may slow down or rest, so they usually do not reach their maximum capacity. It mirrors daily activity rather than testing the limit. || Kyunke patient apni raftar khud chunta hai aur aahista ho sakta hai ya ruk sakta hai, is liye aam taur par woh apni adhikatam salahiyat tak nahi pahunchta. Yeh hadd ko jaanchne ke bajaye rozmarra activity ko dikhata hai.
case: A 62-year-old with COPD walks 310 metres with the Borg score rising from 1 to 5 and oxygen falling from 94 to 90 percent. He stops once for 30 seconds. After eight weeks of exercise he walks 360 metres with a Borg score of 3 and an oxygen low of 92 percent. || A rise of 50 metres is larger than the change usually needed to be sure it is real, and breathlessness is lower, so the benefit is clear. || Share the result with him, set training at a breathlessness of three to four and repeat the test in three months. || At three months he walks 375 metres and manages his shopping without stopping.
safety: **Do not run the test, or stop it,** for chest pain, a recent heart attack or unstable angina, a resting pulse above 120, a very high resting blood pressure, a very low oxygen reading by your unit's limit, dizziness, faintness, a pale or sweaty patient or severe leg pain. Have a chair, a pulse oximeter and a way to call for help ready. || **Test na chalayein, ya rok dein,** seene ke dard, haal ki dil ki bimari ya unstable angina, aaram mein pulse 120 se oopar, bohat zyada aaram ka blood pressure, aap ki unit ki hadd ke mutabiq bohat kam oxygen, chakkar, behoshi jaisi kaifiyat, zard ya paseene mein dooba patient ya shadeed pindli ke dard mein. Kursi, pulse oximeter aur madad bulane ka tareeqa taiyar rakhein.
summary: The six-minute walk test measures how far a patient walks at their own pace; it is submaximal and reflects daily life. || Chhe minute walk test napta hai ke patient apni raftar se kitna chalta hai; yeh submaximal hai aur rozmarra zindagi ko dikhata hai.
summary: Run it the same way every time: same corridor, same words, same encouragement, and record oxygen, heart rate and the Borg score. || Har baar ek tareeqe se chalayein: wohi corridor, wohi alfaz, wohi hausla afzai, aur oxygen, heart rate aur Borg score likhein.
summary: Know the stopping criteria and the size of change that is real (about 30 metres in chronic lung disease); use the result to set exercise intensity. || Rukne ke mayaar aur asli tabdeeli ka size (phephron ki purani bimari mein takreeban 30 meter) jaanein; nateeje se exercise ki shiddat tay karein.
card: What does the six-minute walk test measure? || Chhe minute walk test kya naapta hai? || How far a patient can walk in six minutes at their own pace. || Patient apni raftar se chhe minute mein kitna door chal sakta hai.
card: Why must the test be standardised? || Test standardise kyun hona chahiye? || So a change in distance reflects the patient, not the way the test was done. || Taake distance ki tabdeeli patient ko dikhaye, test ke tareeqe ko nahi.
card: What does the Borg scale record? || Borg scale kya likhta hai? || How hard the patient feels breathing is. || Patient ko saans lena kitna mushkil lagta hai.
card: Name one reason to stop the test. || Test rokne ki ek wajah batayein. || Chest pain, severe breathlessness, dizziness or a pale, sweaty patient. || Seene ka dard, shadeed saans ki tangi, chakkar ya zard paseene mein dooba patient.
